  "finding": "In a line, the base grows by one pitch per arm: two fit the column, three and four do not. The way out is that A's two supports are apart across the conveyor rather than along it, so a second pair standing on the far side reaches the far support at the same distance the present pair reaches the near one. Four arms as two opposed pairs need no extra shoulder line, and each pair keeps a column of its own -- which is what B's two-ended hold and A's turn to the rear supply both depend on. What it costs is a second cell footprint on the far side, which is the same floor the case ② question is about.",
